<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mir Qasim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1777">1777</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Mir Kasim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mir Qasim was Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1760 to 1763, installed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in place of his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munger">Munger</a>, reorganised his army and abolished internal duties, which brought him into conflict with Company traders. Defeated in 1763, he joined the Mughal emperor and the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, but their alliance lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> in 1764.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-qasim.jpg" alt="Mir Qasim" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Qasim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
